---

<ToolPageShell
  slug="dog-chocolate-toxicity-calculator"
  title="Dog Chocolate Toxicity Calculator"
  description="Estimate theobromine exposure from chocolate type and amount vs. dog weight. If your dog ate chocolate, call a vet or poison hotline now."
  answer="If your dog ate chocolate, call your veterinarian, an emergency clinic, the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center at (888) 426-4435, or Pet Poison Helpline at (855) 764-7661 now. This calculator estimates theobromine dose by chocolate type, amount, and weight, but it cannot replace professional advice."
  takeaways={[
    'Emergency first: call a vet or poison control before using any calculator.',
    'Dark and baking chocolate are far more dangerous than milk or white chocolate.',
    'Toxic risk depends on dose per kilogram, so small dogs are at higher risk.',
    'Signs may start 6–12 hours later; do not wait for symptoms.',
    'A low estimate is not a guarantee of safety.',
  ]}
  faq={faq}
  sources={sources}
  adSlotId="tool-after-results-safety"
>
  <ChocolateToxicityCalc slot="calculator" />

  <h2>What to do first</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Remove any remaining chocolate and wrappers from your dog’s reach.</li>
    <li>Call your veterinarian, an emergency clinic, or a poison hotline immediately. Do not wait for symptoms.</li>
    <li>Gather your dog’s weight, the type and amount of chocolate eaten, and when it happened.</li>
    <li>Follow the professional’s instructions. Do not induce vomiting unless told to.</li>
    <li>Use this calculator only as a rough supplement while you are on the phone or traveling to the clinic.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why chocolate is toxic to dogs</h2>
  <p>
    Chocolate contains methylxanthines, mostly theobromine and a smaller amount of caffeine. Humans
    clear theobromine quickly, but dogs eliminate it slowly, so it accumulates and acts on the heart
    and nervous system. The result can range from an upset stomach to tremors, abnormal heart rhythm,
    seizures, and in severe cases death. The risk depends on <em>dose per kilogram</em>, which is why
    the same chocolate bar is a minor event for a 40 kg dog and an emergency for a 3 kg dog.
  </p>

  <h2>Formula: how the calculator estimates risk</h2>
  <p>
    The tool multiplies grams of chocolate by an approximate theobromine content, then divides by your
    dog’s weight to get milligrams per kilogram. It uses approximate values of about 0.01 mg per gram
    for white chocolate, 2 for milk, 5.5 for dark, and 14 for baking chocolate. Those are mid-range
    figures. Real products vary widely with cacao percentage. The result is then matched to commonly
    cited veterinary risk bands:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Theobromine dose</th><th>What may happen</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Under 20 mg/kg</td><td>Often mild or no signs; monitor and call if symptoms appear.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>20–40 mg/kg</td><td>Vomiting, restlessness, increased heart rate possible.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>40–60 mg/kg</td><td>Moderate to severe signs, including cardiac effects.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over 60 mg/kg</td><td>Seizures and life-threatening signs possible; emergency.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A 10 kg (22 lb) dog eats 100 g of milk chocolate (about a large bar). Theobromine ≈ 100 × 2 =
    200 mg, so the dose is 20 mg/kg, right at the edge of the mild-to-moderate range. The same dog
    eating 100 g of dark chocolate gets 550 mg, or 55 mg/kg, in the moderate-to-severe range and a
    clear emergency. A 40 kg dog eating the same 100 g of milk chocolate receives just 5 mg/kg, which
    is likely a mild event, though it is still sensible to call.
  </p>

  <h2>Symptoms to watch for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Vomiting or diarrhea, often within a few hours.</li>
    <li>Restlessness, hyperactivity, excessive panting, or increased thirst and urination.</li>
    <li>Rapid, irregular, or racing heartbeat.</li>
    <li>Muscle tremors, stiffness, or seizures.</li>
    <li>Collapse, which is a life-threatening emergency.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Symptoms often start six to twelve hours after ingestion and can last a day or longer, because
    theobromine is cleared slowly. Dogs with heart disease are at greater risk at lower doses.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond theobromine: other risks in chocolate products</h2>
  <p>
    Chocolate products can contain other hazards. Sugar-free sweets may contain xylitol, which can
    cause dangerous low blood sugar and liver failure. Raisins, macadamia nuts, coffee beans, and
    large amounts of fat can also cause problems, including pancreatitis. Tell the poison hotline
    exactly what was in the product. Our <a href="/guides/chocolate-and-dogs-safety/">chocolate and
    dogs safety guide</a> covers prevention and what to keep in your home emergency plan.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits of this calculator</h2>
  <p>
    The tool is a simplified education aid. It does not know the exact product, how much cacao it
    contains, whether your dog vomited, your dog’s health history, or when ingestion occurred. A
    low estimate does not mean no treatment is needed, and a high estimate does not predict the
    outcome. Professionals can use treatment such as activated charcoal, IV fluids, and heart
    monitoring, and timing matters. See also the <a href="/tools/dog-water-intake-calculator/">water
    intake calculator</a> for hydration context and the <a href="/tools/dog-bmi-calculator/">body
    condition helper</a> for general health tracking.
  </p>
</ToolPageShell>
